**The First Mirror**

Long ago, when the world was young, a vain goddess named Selara ruled the mountain peaks. She was beautiful beyond measure, but no one could tell her so—for she lived alone, and the earth had not yet learned to reflect.

One day, a clever mortal named Kael climbed her mountain, seeking wisdom. Selara demanded, "Tell me, am I not the fairest of all?"

Kael replied honestly, "I cannot say, for I have never seen you and another together to compare."

Enraged, Selara hurled a silver disc at him. But Kael, quick-witted, polished the disc until it gleamed. When he held it up, Selara gasped—for the first time, she saw her own face.

She stared for days, transfixed. The seasons changed, but Selara could not look away. Her vanity trapped her within the reflection.

Taking pity, Kael shattered the disc into a thousand pieces and scattered them across the world—into lakes, into polished stones, into pools of water. 

"Now," he said, "beauty belongs to everyone who seeks it, and no one may hoard it alone."

And that is why we find our reflections everywhere, yet nowhere can we gaze at ourselves forever.
