Myth: Long ago, when the world was new, a proud king named Aldric ruled a vast kingdom. He possessed everything—gold, armies, palaces—yet he felt incomplete, for he had never seen his own face.

"Bring me my reflection!" he commanded his subjects. But mirrors had not yet been created.

A humble potter named Mira heard his decree. She climbed the king's tower carrying a bowl of still water. "Look here, Your Majesty," she said.

Aldric peered into the bowl and saw himself for the first time. He was captivated, staring for hours. Days passed. He refused to eat or sleep, mesmerized by his own image.

Mira grew worried. "Your Majesty, you're wasting away."

"But I must see myself!" he cried.

Compassionately, Mira tipped the bowl, spilling the water. It flowed down the tower, splitting into streams and rivers across the land.

"Now," she said gently, "your reflection lives in every brook and pond. You may visit it, but you cannot possess it. Beauty must be shared, not hoarded."

Aldric understood. He left his tower and walked among his people, finally seeing not just himself, but the world around him reflected in a thousand waters.
