Myth: In ancient times, when the world was young, a wealthy merchant named Darian traveled everywhere seeking the rarest treasure. He had silks from distant lands, jewels from deep mines, spices from hidden islands—yet satisfaction eluded him.

"Find me something no one else possesses!" he demanded of a wandering sage.

The sage smiled and handed him an empty crystal vial. "Fill this with your own reflection."

Darian laughed mockingly, but tried anyway. He held the vial to polished bronze, to calm pools, to silver plates—yet no reflection would enter the crystal.

Years passed. His fortune dwindled as he obsessed over this impossible task. Finally, broken and alone, Darian sat by a mountain stream, weeping.

His tears fell into the vial. Looking down, he saw his face captured in the water of his own sorrow—older, humbler, transformed by his journey.

The sage appeared again. "Now you understand. The rarest treasure cannot be hoarded or possessed. It must be earned through change."

Darian poured the vial into the stream, watching his reflection flow away to join countless others.

"True wealth," the sage said, "is knowing when to let go."

And Darian finally found peace in empty hands.
